{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-8d4eec92a0b33278", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\tExplainable AI through the Lens of Material\n2\tAgency: Enabling Musical Interface Design with\n3\tNeural Audio Models\n4\t\n5\tarXiv:2607.23309v1 [cs.HC] 25 Jul 2026\n6\t\n7\tShuoyang Jasper Zheng, Anna Xambó Sedó, and Nick Bryan-Kinns\n8\t\n9\tUnder review for “Explainable AI for the Arts” (N. Bryan-Kinns, Ed.), Springer.\n10\tAbstract Recent work in Human-Computer Interaction (HCI) increasingly treats AI\n11\tmodels as design materials that have distinctive computational properties to shape\n12\tdesign artifacts. Artists learn to work with the model “at play” to explore their emerging\n13\tproperties. The aim of explainability, in this view, is to make visible a crafting and\n14\thacking space to enable sustained creative practices with AI. In this chapter, we propose\n15\tmaterial explainability as a range of activities and artifacts that transform AI models\n16\tinto accessible and inclusive design materials in the workspace of artists, designers, and\n17\tmakers. We present a case study of building a repository of resources to enable artistic\n18\texplorations of neural audio models in New Interfaces for Musical Expression (NIME)\n19\tdesign. Reflecting on our community-building journey and the making of a collection\n20\tof musical interface designs with a group of artists, we raise three recommendations\n21\ton enabling the exploration of AI as materials in artistic practices to inspire future XAI\n22\tdesign for artists.\n23\tKeywords AI as Material · Musical Interface Design · Neural Audio Models\n24\t\n25\t1 Introduction\n26\tThe materiality of interaction design, in Human-Computer Interaction (HCI), emphasizes that the emergent characteristics of design materials can drive the design process\n27\tShuoyang Jasper Zheng\n28\tCentre for Digital Music, Queen Mary University of London, London, United Kingdom\n29\te-mail: shuoyang.zheng@qmul.ac.uk\n30\tAnna Xambó Sedó\n31\tCentre for Digital Music, Queen Mary University of London, London, United Kingdom\n32\tNick Bryan-Kinns\n33\tCreative Computing Institute, University of the Arts London, London, United Kingdom\n34\t\n35\t1\n36\t\n37\t\f2\n38\t\n39\tSJ. Zheng et al.\n40\t\n41\t[32]. Competent designers learn a rich material understanding of the grain, resistances,\n42\tand propensities of materials in their workspace through hands-on practices, and develop\n43\ttechnical know-how to work with them [36]. In recent discourse in AI and HCI, a trend\n44\tof acknowledging AI models as design materials is taking place [30]. Technological\n45\tadvances are thereby situated in creative practices in which designers probe, configure,\n46\tand appropriate them as materials to unpack their creative possibilities [10], and craft\n47\tinteractive experiences rather than solely focusing on engineering functionalities.\n48\tThis material-oriented perspective offers a practice-based lens to XAIxArts, through\n49\twhich explainability can be conceived as working with AIs as materials to understand\n50\tthem “at play”, through probing, configuring, and tweaking [3]. In this way, the audience for explainability is shifted to artists, designers, and makers, and the aim is to\n51\tmake visible the crafting and hacking space for practice-based exploration with AIs.\n52\tTherefore, we propose material explainability – that is, a range of activities or artifacts\n53\tthat aim to transform AI models into accessible and inclusive design materials, to enable\n54\tsustained creative practices with AI materials.\n55\tIn this chapter, we discuss the intersection of explainability and the material-oriented\n56\tengagement with AI models in creative practices. We reflect on a case study of New\n57\tInterfaces for Musical Expression (NIME) designs with AI models as materials. The\n58\tcase study focuses on a specific domain of machine learning that relies on explainability\n59\t– the latent space, data representations learned by AI models that have typically been\n60\ttreated as a “black box” that lacks explainability. We show how we build a repository\n61\tof resources, including a software toolkit, documentation, tutorials, and a shared space\n62\tfor projects to bring latent space into the workspace of artists, designers, and makers,\n63\tand how they explore the materiality aspect of latent space to craft musical interfaces.\n64\t\n65\t2 Towards Explainability for Accessible and Inclusive AI Materials\n66\tWe take the broader view of XAIxArts to refer to explainability as understanding\n67\thow to work with AI models in action in artistic contexts [37, 44]. In literature such\n68\tas XAI in large language models [13], this way of gaining functional and operational\n69\tunderstanding of the model, typically referred to as around the edges explanations, aims\n70\tto “provide insights into a black box that can afford a wider range of actions” [13, p. 8]. In\n71\tartistic practices with AIs, a similar “understanding models in action” approach has been\n72\twidely adopted. For instance, dancers exploring the model’s parameter space through\n73\tembodied movements to relate its outputs to their actions [24], musicians creating\n74\tannotations for sound-producing gestures to play the model as a musical instrument\n75\t[45], or visual artists engaging in technical practice with the model to discover ways of\n76\tsculpting artistic expressions [12]. A commonality, as described by Scurto et al. [30]\n77\tusing the term “crafting machine learning”, is that artists seek in-depth engagement\n78\twith their AI models to discover practical necessities of how to work with them, beyond\n79\ta technical XAI lens. With this view of craft and design, and the iterative cycles of\n80\tprobing, tuning, and programming models as computational material, it is reasonable\n81\tto look at the “material turn” in HCI to unpack the role of explainability.\n82\t\n83\t\fExplainable AI through the Lens of Material Agency\n84\t\n85\t3\n86\t\n87\t2.1 The Material Turn and Material Agency\n88\tThere has been a “material turn” in HCI and interaction design research over the\n89\tpast fifteen years [17] – that is, a material understanding of how distinctive properties\n90\tof information technologies shape interaction experience and possibility. Influential\n91\tcharacterizations of this material lens have guided research such as computational\n92\tcomposites [35] which treat code, electronics, and physical matter as tightly coupled\n93\tcomposites, and material-centered interaction design [36] in which designers learn a\n94\trepertoire of materials and their grain, resistances, and propensities through hands-on\n95\twork, in a similar way as they would work with wood, textiles, or sound. HCI researchers\n96\tuse material agency to describe how emerging properties of materials actively contribute\n97\tto the way a design activity unfolds. In this view, agency is “something that emerges and\n98\tis dynamically enacted through practice” [32, p. 2506], rather than as a pre-existing set\n99\tof affordances of a particular actor [4]. The notion of intra-action [2] is widely adopted\n100\tto question the extreme localization of agency – either material dominates the human\n101\tor human dominates the material – instead, musical interactions are co-produced in the\n102\tdesigner’s entangled practice with their materials [23].\n103\tIn recent discourse of AI in interaction design, a similar trend of acknowledging the\n104\tcomputational properties of AI as materials is taking place, which we describe below.\n105\t\n106\t2.2 AI as Design Material\n107\tScurto et al. [30] introduce elements of Machine Learning (ML) that constitute its\n108\tmateriality, including techniques, data, models, and algorithms. They argue that artbased ML crafting can take these technical elements as an entry point for design, and\n109\temphasize on “the experience of raw computational properties through incremental\n110\tcrafting” [30, p. 2022] rather than solely on engineering functionalities. The materiality\n111\tof AI is not a singular form of infrastructure or software. Instead, it extends to a network\n112\tof actors beyond just artists, audience, and the models [38].\n113\tTherefore, the material agency of AI systems in art and design has been described as a\n114\theterogeneous assemblage that involves a range of “technically, materially, socially, and\n115\ttemporally” distributed infrastructures [18]. To be clear, one strand of research in HCI on\n116\thuman-AI co-creation focuses on explicitly assigning agency to either the AI or human\n117\t[43]. This contrasts the intra-action relationship in which AI models are understood\n118\tas material rather than co-creator [32], and the material agency only emerges when\n119\tbeing surfaced, negotiated, and repurposed. Acknowledging the material agency of AI\n120\tmodels means attending to how their affordances, tendencies, and constraints participate\n121\tin shaping the artifacts and aesthetic commitments, and being able to design, craft, and\n122\trefine them across time [30].\n123\tRepositioning AI in this material agency perspective implies that its characteristic\n124\tbehaviors are not merely technical constraints to be overcome, but affordances that\n125\tdesigners must learn to work with through practice [39]. In this respect, Pelinski et al.\n126\t\n127\t\f4\n128\t\n129\tSJ. Zheng et al.\n130\t\n131\t[27, p. 10] propose technical practice research to account for the material agency in\n132\tworks that are surrounded by technical development:\n133\tWe often do exist in a more dialogic process with our technical practice, in which we converse\n134\twith our materials, and as such, finding space for practice research might allow us to articulate\n135\tknowledge about how (rather than what) we do.\n136\t\n137\tTechnical practice research argues that the material-oriented perspective can be\n138\tneglected when knowledge production is driven by searching for insights and achieving\n139\tgoals. In practice, the real-time and reflexive accounts during the technical development\n140\tphase play an important role in shedding light on the temporal emergence of material\n141\tagency [27]. The issue of linear problem-solving narratives in practice research mirrors\n142\tthe challenge of XAI in the Arts [7]. In particular, technical explanations are typically\n143\tframed as goal and productivity oriented, raising the barrier to appropriating them for\n144\tcreative and artistic practices [6].\n145\t\n146\t2.3 Accessible and Inclusive XAI Materials\n147\tIn the landscape of XAIxArts, attention has been drawn to the accessible, inclusive,\n148\tinterdisciplinary practice with AI [7]. This echoes the interest in diversifying dissemination methods in HCI [31], which argues that traditional academic publications as a\n149\tway of sharing research findings can exclude wider audiences. Yoo et al. [41] propose\n150\tAlternative Research Outcomes to challenge the one-size-fits-all research dissemination\n151\tstrategies. They explore situated and dedicated research activities or artifacts, such as\n152\tdocumentaries, public exhibitions, multimedia artifacts, as “new endpoints to translate,\n153\tcommunicate, or disseminate research insights in an accessible and engaging form\n154\ttailored to the intended audience” [41].\n155\tPractitioners in the XAIxArts community also adopt the view of making explainability engaging to audiences with a range of technical literacy. This is reflected as an AI\n156\tas Material perspective [5] in which the AI models themselves can be artworks. Artistled practices such as probing model behaviors, exposing bias, and re-creating glitches\n157\temerge to enable engagement with a wider audience. In this way, research insights on\n158\tmodel explainability are made accessible through alternative research outcomes such\n159\tas exhibitions, interactive experiences, or artistic installations.\n160\tThe XAIxArts discourse on exploring the AI itself as artistic pieces poses open\n161\tquestions on where the creative agency resides [5, p. 45], aiming to answer “who is\n162\tresponsible for the explanation: the AI model, the artist, or the audience?” Here, we\n163\tsuggest that it is desirable to consider the dissemination of models into the hands of\n164\tartists. Therefore, the material explainability aims to answer how to enable artists to\n165\tdevelop technical know-how and technical practices with AI materials. A similar notion\n166\tof material explainability was raised in the XAIxArts Manifesto [7], which calls for\n167\t“demystifying the computational materiality of AI in accessible languages and engaging\n168\tformats” for inclusive dissemination of AI as material.\n169\t\n170\t\fExplainable AI through the Lens of Material Agency\n171\t\n172\t5\n173\t\n174\tFollowing this call for material explainability in a concrete creative-technical setting,\n175\twe focus on neural audio models in musical interface design, where AI models are both\n176\ta key site of creative potential and a barrier to hands-on engagement.\n177\t\n178\t2.4 Neural Audio Models in New Interfaces for Musical Expression\n179\tTo unpack this theme and illustrate the role of explainability in exploring the materiality\n180\tof AI, we look at a specific domain of generative AI for sound synthesis that is typically\n181\tconsidered to lack explainability – the latent space. In AI for sound analysis and\n182\tsynthesis, the latent space is a compact multi-dimensional map learned by neural audio\n183\tmodels. A typical implementation of neural audio models is an autoencoder, in which an\n184\tencoder compresses audio signals into a latent space, and a decoder reconstructs audio\n185\tfrom latent representations. This method of learning audio data allows them to capture\n186\tcomplex timbral and spectral characteristics that are difficult to specify explicitly. We\n187\tdirect intrigued readers to the chapter by Yee-King [40] for a comprehensive and\n188\tinclusive explanation of using latent spaces in creative practices.\n189\tIn New Interfaces for Musical Expression (NIME), neural audio models open new\n190\topportunities by repositioning sound synthesis as a matter of interacting with the learned\n191\tlatent representations. For instance, the latent space walk approach of mapping user\n192\tinputs into latent spaces enables designers to craft novel musical instruments [28, 45].\n193\tThis shifts neural audio models from a technical artifact into design materials that can\n194\tbe explored, used, and appropriated.\n195\tDespite the creative possibilities it opens, one of the long-standing criticisms of\n196\tlatent spaces is the absence of easy-to-understand musical attributes encoded across\n197\tall dimensions. Consequently, the ways of navigating the latent space and relating the\n198\tsound outputs rely on the designer’s sense-making. Therefore, to explore neural audio\n199\tmodels in NIME design, designers seek to understand the model’s computational properties through iterative exploration and configuration. However, technical AI research\n200\tis typically positioned outside creative coding and audio production environments, and\n201\tlimited learning resources raise the barrier to hands-on engagement with the model\n202\t[11]. In the next section, we present our journey of transforming neural audio models\n203\tinto situated and easy-to-integrate materials in the hands of artists and designers.\n204\t\n205\t3 Crafting Musical Interactions with AI Audio Latent Space\n206\tThis section describes a case study of building a community of resources, including\n207\tsoftware tools, documentation, tutorials, and demo projects, to turn neural audio models\n208\tinto explainable materials for designers. We introduce the Latent Terrain toolkit, a\n209\tpackage of Max/MSP objects to build customized interactive sound spaces for neural\n210\taudio autoencoders. We acknowledge that the appropriation of technical artifacts can\n211\tbe open-ended and cannot be contained by a singular effort. Therefore, we hope to use\n212\tour project only as a heuristic for further work, rather than an ultimate solution.\n213\t\n214\t\f6\n215\t\n216\tSJ. Zheng et al.\n217\t\n218\tFig. 1 We held a series of workshops in which musicians were invited to explore the DMI probe\n219\t(Right). They were tasked to create musical scores to re-enact sound pieces (Left).\n220\t\n221\t3.1 Description of the Research Project\n222\tLatent Terrain is the output of the first author’s doctoral research project conducted\n223\tbetween 2024 and 2025 [45], at the intersection of NIME, XAI, and HCI. The research\n224\tlooked at the embodied music cognition aspect of movement-sound interaction in\n225\taudio latent spaces, investigating how musicians explore the latent space as a musical\n226\tinstrument and the performing techniques that emerged. A full technical account of the\n227\tproject is beyond the scope of this paper, we therefore invite readers to our previous work\n228\tfor a detailed journey of the research [45]. A brief narrative of the journey includes:\n229\t1. The research took the method of Digital Musical Instruments (DMIs) as probes [19],\n230\tin which musical instruments are built to serve as a means to explore musicians’\n231\tsubjective experience.\n232\t2. We crafted a DMI with a stylus and tablet interface (shown in Figure 1) that allows\n233\tmusicians to navigate the latent space by operating the stylus, and hear the real-time\n234\tsound synthesized by the autoencoder.\n235\t3. Latent Terrain was developed as a deep learning method to interactively map the\n236\tlatent space of an autoencoder to a 2-dimensional control space, using a customized\n237\taudio corpora. To facilitate rapid prototyping and testing of the mapping, we developed a toolkit in the Max/MSP visual programming environment that allows us to\n238\tintegrate the Latent Terrain model and the autoencoder together into the DMI.\n239\t\n240\t3.2 Bridging Computational Materiality to Accessible Platforms\n241\tInitially, the Latent Terrain toolkit was published as supplementary material in a research\n242\tarticle [45]. As the project progressed, the intended audience shifted from academic\n243\tresearchers to broader DMI makers in the field of AI audio synthesis. This motivated\n244\t\n245\t\fExplainable AI through the Lens of Material Agency\n246\t\n247\t7\n248\t\n249\tFig. 2 A collection of materials to disseminate the research aspect of Latent Terrain to hands-on\n250\texperience in Max/MSP: (A) Online documentation, (B) video tutorials, (C and D) example patches\n251\tas part of a getting-started guide.\n252\t\n253\tus to maintain the toolkit as a public Max/MSP (a visual programming language for\n254\tmusic and multimedia) package 1 to allow others to create their own instruments,\n255\trather than offering a static musical instrument with a fixed mode of interaction. In\n256\tthis way, the Latent Terrain system became a computational material, working together\n257\twith autoencoders, customized audio corpora, and interfaces, in the ecology of musical\n258\tinteractions design.\n259\tThe intention of the project, therefore, became transforming the technical system into\n260\tan accessible and easy-to-integrate material in DMI design. We packaged the mapping\n261\tmodel into a collection of Max/MSP externals, offering a Max-style API that supports\n262\tthe workflow of model creation and inference. The API design was situated and guided\n263\tby the first author’s practice of DMI design and composing with autoencoders, which\n264\twe reported in another publication [45]. In summary, we reflected on a long-term selfuse of the system in a series of interface designs to identify the practical necessities in\n265\tthe workflow of integrating the latent space into DMI. The aim was to expose proper\n266\tconfigurations to the API to allow for customizations and extended uses of the system,\n267\twhile maintaining simplicity to avoid technical knowledge becoming a barrier.\n268\t1 Our Latent Terrain Toolkit: https://jasper-zheng.github.io/nn_terrain/\n269\t\n270\t\f8\n271\t\n272\tSJ. Zheng et al.\n273\t\n274\tTo share our technical know-how gained throughout the development process, we\n275\tmaintain an online repository2 of documentation and tutorials as a form of alternative\n276\tresearch outcomes [41], shown in Figure 2. The intended audience is audio programmers, musicians, DMI makers, and broader communities of creative practitioners. The\n277\tonline documentation provides an introduction to the package and a getting-started\n278\tguide, using inclusive language to explain the inner architecture of autoencoders and\n279\tentry points to unpack them. It also contains step-by-step instructions with video tutorials to cover a range of use cases of the package, from basic workflows such as\n280\tloading and visualizing pre-trained mapping models, to in-depth customization such as\n281\tcreating one’s own mapping model and experimenting with other input devices. On the\n282\tMax/MSP side, each object in the package offers a help file containing a walkthrough of\n283\tits functionalities, explanations of the API and configurations, and working examples\n284\tof how it can be used in a patch.\n285\tWe also maintain an open repository of autoencoders to enrich the pool of materials\n286\tfor the designers (links to these resources can be found on our online documentation\n287\tpage). This is motivated by the gap between research on autoencoders and their practical\n288\tavailability in musical platforms. The Realtime Audio Variational autoEncoder (RAVE)\n289\t[8] model is widely adopted by practitioners in NIME due to its availability to be used\n290\twith the nn∼3 external in Max/MSP, and several other creative coding platforms, for\n291\treal-time inference. However, a range of other neural audio autoencoders is exclusive to\n292\tresearch environments, and can lack the consideration of real-time streamability at the\n293\tdevelopment stage [9]. We added two other open-source autoencoders (Music2Latent\n294\t[26] and the autoencoder in the Stable Audio Open 1.0 model [14]) that vary in configurations and model architecture, and implemented them to the nn∼ external. We also\n295\tmade available a collection of RAVE models trained by the first author using Creative\n296\tCommons datasets. This repository of resources aims to encourage a plural repertoire\n297\tof materials, and to bridge the capability of recent research in neural audio synthesis to\n298\tcreative coding environments.\n299\t\n300\t3.3 Community Building with Artists\n301\tTo further encourage artistic exploration of the repository we created, we took a\n302\tcommunity-driven approach of allowing artists, designers, and digital luthiers to share\n303\ttheir projects and technical know-how. The benefit of communities that intersect researchers, academic knowledge, and audiences has been recognised in various art and\n304\ttechnology projects, such as the interactive machine learning tool Wekinator [15], the\n305\tcorpora manipulation toolkit FluCoMa [34, 33]. Inspired by these works, we create a\n306\tspace on our website 4 for blog posts and project showcases, and invited a group of\n307\tartists to explore a key question of “how do neural audio models open a domain of\n308\tdesign in musical interfaces”.\n309\t2 https://jasper-zheng.github.io/nn_terrain/\n310\t3 https://github.com/acids-ircam/nn_tilde\n311\t4 https://jasper-zheng.github.io/nn_terrain/posts/\n312\t\n313\t\fExplainable AI through the Lens of Material Agency\n314\t\n315\t9\n316\t\n317\tFig. 3 A collection of projects made using the Latent Terrain toolkit: (A) A MaxMSP interface for using\n318\tvisual sketches to steer a RAVE model; (B) A step sequencer that plays short “latent samples” while\n319\tallowing users to adapt the terrain sound space on the fly. (C) nn/mémoire by Jiatong Liu, an audiovisual virtual gallery with a theme of capturing the sound of cultural heritage in Hutongs (traditional\n320\tnorthern Chinese courtyard houses in Beijing); (D) Trek by Nico Garcı́a-Peguinho and Nikhil Bullock, a\n321\tcollaborative composition and live performance system exploring sampling and interpolation strategies\n322\tfor the latent space; (E) Repressive Terrain by Keigo Yoshida, an experimental data sonification patch\n323\twith a theme of active interventions in a focused meditation; (F) ambient terrain 1 by Dan Hearn, an\n324\tambient soundtrack exploring the combination of AI and non-AI materials in the sample-based music\n325\tproduction workflow.\n326\t\n327\tFigure 3 shows the collection of works in the repository. The first two projects (A\n328\tand B), created by the authors as an initiation to explore the design space. The other\n329\tfour projects (C to F) are contributed by four artists who shared a mutual interest\n330\tin exploring neural audio synthesis. Each project in the collection took a different\n331\tapproach to musical interface design, ranging from an immersive virtual gallery, a live\n332\tperformance, a brain–computer interaction, and an ambient soundscape composition.\n333\t\n334\t\f10\n335\t\n336\tSJ. Zheng et al.\n337\t\n338\tHere we share reflections from the four artists to gain insight into their approach\n339\tto understanding the models during their creation process. Quotes are taken from our\n340\tconversations with each artist during the creation of the collection.\n341\tVarious artists acknowledged the uniqueness of working with neural audio models\n342\tby comparing them to conventional sound synthesis algorithms. For instance, Jiatong\n343\tLiu, a creative technologist in machine learning, composition, and film, the creator\n344\tof nn/mémoire, made a comparison of neural audio models and conventional music\n345\tproduction tools such as reverb or EQ: “[neural audio models] shape a sound by entirely\n346\tchanging the structure and order of it”. Keigo Yoshida, an artist and scientist, the\n347\tcreator of Repressive Terrain, emphasizes the interactive adaptation of sound spaces as\n348\tan important iterative way of customising and working with the AI material. He made\n349\tan analogy of adding training data to the Latent Terrain system with adding materials\n350\tto an audio sampler, but “it is able to shift timbre and musical structure across genres”.\n351\tNico Garcı́a-Peguinho, an artist and researcher in machine listening and algorithmic\n352\tcomposition, the co-creator of Trek, highlighted that “[neural audio synthesis] feels less\n353\tintuitive than conventional oscillator-based synthesis”.\n354\tArtists also mention the importance of learning from trials and errors. Dan Hearn,\n355\ta creative technologist and musician, the creator of ambient terrain 1, describes his\n356\tworkflow with autoencoders for soundscape composition as “explorative and serendipitous,” emphasising that the autoencoder “gradually finds its place” within his existing\n357\tsample-based practice through repeated trials, rather than being adopted as a standalone instrument with fixed affordances. Jiatong describes how working with neural\n358\taudio models demands a shift in composition approach: “need to learn to deal with the\n359\tunpredictability through trial and error, without fully eliminating uncertainty”. Nico\n360\talso highlighted that a tacit understanding of how to work with the material was made\n361\tlegible through active and embodied listening in the sound space. Keigo described the\n362\t“tracing the map like a musical score” as a compositional method that is difficult to\n363\tarticulate without hands-on engagement.\n364\tThere is also a demand for tools and facilities to support the hands-on engagement\n365\twith neural audio models. For instance, Nico mentioned that conventional sound synthesis methods, such as sample-based synthesis or oscillator-based synthesis, benefit\n366\tfrom a rich set of tools such as visualisation or preset saving and loading, whereas for\n367\tneural audio models, “very often you have to build things yourself, which makes them\n368\tharder to work with”.\n369\t\n370\t4 Enabling Artists’ Exploration of AI Materials\n371\tBased on the development process of the Latent Terrain package, the online resources,\n372\tand the reflections from the artists, here we discuss three takeaways on how to transform\n373\tAI models into accessible and inclusive XAI materials in the crafting space of NIME,\n374\tto inspire future XAI design and development.\n375\tFirst, make visible how AI models can be tailored into the material assemblage of different artists’ practices. In NIME design, designers work with a range\n376\tof synthesis algorithms, sensors, and the listening and composing practice, forming an\n377\t\n378\t\fExplainable AI through the Lens of Material Agency\n379\t\n380\t11\n381\t\n382\tassemblage [18]. Composing and weaving together a set of co-functioning materials in\n383\tthe assemblage is through the rapid cycles of trial and adjustment. Reflecting on the four\n384\tcreative projects, this iterative way of exploring and integrating materials is enabled\n385\tby conditions in which they can probe, tune, and configure the AI model. In this way,\n386\tthe role of autoencoders in creative practices became clear through the connection and\n387\tinteraction with other materials in the design space, rather than through predetermined\n388\taffordances. In contrast to AI models that are constrained in technical research environments or API calls, the Latent Terrain toolkit unpacks the latent space into a creative\n389\tcoding environment, in which the autoencoder can belong to a broad constellation of\n390\tAI and non-AI materials. The use and appropriation of AI as material can therefore be\n391\texplored and understood by creative makers.\n392\tSecond, enable artists to learn technical and practical know-how of AI models\n393\tthrough crafting. DMI design, as a creative practice, is interdisciplinary technical work\n394\tthat involves apprenticeships and learning curves [42]. Technical practice research is\n395\ttermed to relocate knowledge production in first-person, real-time technical practices\n396\t[27]. This echoes the view of material-centered design, in which material understanding\n397\tis built through close and iterative engagement, and enables skills of weaving together\n398\tmaterials to fulfill a piece of design [36].\n399\tExplainability, in this respect, is not a one-time account of the model’s inner work\n400\tthat will be disposed of immediately after the generation, but resources that can ensure\n401\tmakers’ sustained practices [11] with the AI material. Within the four artistic pieces,\n402\tmakers do not shy away from tuning and troubleshooting their AI models. Instead,\n403\tthe complex and technical nature of latent space is acknowledged, but as described by\n404\tJiatong, they “learn to deal with the unpredictability”, and by Nico, they “seed help from\n405\tthe documentation to learn things like visualizing the latent parameters”. Crucially, this\n406\tform of explainability is described as tacit understanding [1] that is uniquely attached\n407\tbetween the maker and the material [21]. This makes explainability inseparable from\n408\thands-on experimentation and listening to enable technique learning.\n409\tThird, foster exchanges of domain-specific techniques and know-how among\n410\tartists through community-building. In creative practices, techniques and knowledge\n411\temerge when communities can repeatedly return to the same material to negotiate and\n412\tcollaborate [29]. This ocio-material environments [25] is enabled by allowing practitioners to establish domain-specific vocabularies to share experiences and techniques.\n413\tFor instance, the account of the turntable is an example of how sustained practice and\n414\tmusical culture transform a technical artifact into a playable medium [20]. In the domain\n415\tof AI for creativity, various works argue that “providing the tools is not enough” [22].\n416\tInstead, the long-term and techno-fluent engagements with AI as material are enabled\n417\tby shareable resources for learning and pedagogy [16, 22]. Within the practices with\n418\tthe Latent Terrain toolkit, artists discover their ways of knowing and working that can\n419\tinfluence other practitioners. For instance, Nico’s use of “active listening” method and\n420\tDan’s use of “latent resampling” to characterize his workflow. This community aspect\n421\tof explainability focuses on how the tacit knowledge of crafting and working can be\n422\tarticulated and circulated through communities to ensure a sustained practice with AI\n423\tmaterials.\n424\t\n425\t\f12\n426\t\n427\tSJ. Zheng et al.\n428\t\n429\t5 Conclusion\n430\tIn this chapter, we suggest that XAI for creative practice can be approached as material\n431\texplainability: a range of activities and artifacts that aim to bring AI models into the\n432\tworkspace as accessible and inclusive materials for design. Using latent space in neural\n433\taudio models as a case study, we described how the Latent Terrain toolkit translates a\n434\ttechnically complex and opaque representational space in AI models into a repository\n435\tof software tools, documentation, resources, and tutorials for artists. Based on the\n436\trepository and the reflection from a community of artists using it, we show how the\n437\tmaterial explainability approach supports hands-on artistic explorations of neural audio\n438\tmodels in NIME design. We concluded by raising three suggestions on future XAI\n439\tdesign for artistic practices: enable crafting and hacking by making visible how AI\n440\tmodels can be tailored into different artists’ practices; enable artists to learn technical\n441\tand practical know-how of AI models through crafting; foster exchanges of domainspecific techniques and know-how among artists through community-building.\n442\tAcknowledgements Shuoyang Zheng is a research student supported by the EPSRC UKRI Centre\n443\tfor Doctoral Training in Artificial Intelligence and Music (grant number EP/S022694/1). We thank all\n444\tfour artists for their engagement with the project and their contributions of their project descriptions,\n445\tgraphic content, and blog posts on the project webpage.\n446\t\n447\tEthics Approval The project was approved by the Queen Mary University of London ethics committee,\n448\treference number: QMERC20.565.DSEECS25.026.\n449\t\n450\tReferences\n451\t[1] Abuzuraiq, A.M., Pasquier, P.: Explainability-in-Action: Enabling Expressive Manipulation and Tacit Understanding by Bending Diffusion Models in ComfyUI.\n452\tIn: Proceedings of Explainable AI for the Arts Workshop 2025 (XAIxArts 2025)\n453\t(2025)\n454\t[2] Barad, K.: Meeting the Universe Halfway: Quantum Physics and the Entanglement\n455\tof Matter and Meaning. Duke University Press (2007)\n456\t[3] Benjamin, J.J., Berger, A., Merrill, N., Pierce, J.: Machine Learning Uncertainty as\n457\ta Design Material: A Post-Phenomenological Inquiry. In: Proceedings of the 2021\n458\tCHI Conference on Human Factors in Computing Systems, CHI ’21. Association\n459\tfor Computing Machinery, New York, NY, USA (2021)\n460\t[4] Bown, O., Eldridge, A., Mccormack, J.: Understanding interaction in contemporary digital music: From instruments to behavioural objects. Organised Sound\n461\t14(2), 188–196 (2009)\n462\t[5] Bryan-Kinns, N.: Reflections on Explainable AI for the Arts (XAIxArts). Interactions 31(1), 43–47 (2024)\n463\t\n464\t\fExplainable AI through the Lens of Material Agency\n465\t\n466\t13\n467\t\n468\t[6] Bryan-Kinns, N., Li, Z.: Reducing Barriers to the Use of Marginalised Music\n469\tGenres in AI. In: Proceedings of Explainable AI for the Arts Workshop 2024\n470\t(XAIxArts 2024) (2024)\n471\t[7] Bryan-Kinns, N., Zheng, S.J., Castro, F., Lewis, M., Chang, J.R., Vigliensoni, G.,\n472\tBroad, T., Clemens, M.P., Wilson, E.: XAIxArts Manifesto: Explainable AI for\n473\tthe Arts. In: Proceedings of the Extended Abstracts of the CHI Conference on\n474\tHuman Factors in Computing Systems, CHI EA ’25. Association for Computing\n475\tMachinery, New York, NY, USA (2025)\n476\t[8] Caillon, A., Esling, P.: RAVE: A variational autoencoder for fast and high-quality\n477\tneural audio synthesis (2021)\n478\t[9] Caillon, A., Esling, P.: Streamable Neural Audio Synthesis With Non-Causal\n479\tConvolutions (2022)\n480\t[10] Caramiaux, B., Fdili Alaoui, S.: “Explorers of Unknown Planets”: Practices and\n481\tPolitics of Artificial Intelligence in Visual Arts. Proc. ACM Hum.-Comput.\n482\tInteract. 6(CSCW2) (2022)\n483\t[11] Clarke, I., Rı́, F.A.D., Masu, R.: Longevity of Deep Generative Models in NIME:\n484\tChallenges and Practices for Reactivation. In: D. Cavdir, F. Berthaut (eds.) Proceedings of the International Conference on New Interfaces for Musical Expression, pp. 224–230. Canberra, Australia (2025)\n485\t[12] Cole, A., Grierson, M.: Kiss/Crash: Using Diffusion Models to Explore Real\n486\tDesire in the Shadow of Artificial Representations. Proc. ACM Comput. Graph.\n487\tInteract. Tech. 6(2) (2023)\n488\t[13] Ehsan, U., Riedl, M.: Explainable AI Reloaded: Challenging the XAI Status Quo\n489\tin the Era of Large Language Models. In: Proceedings of the Halfway to the\n490\tFuture Symposium, HttF ’24. Association for Computing Machinery, New York,\n491\tNY, USA (2024)\n492\t[14] Evans, Z., Parker, J.D., Carr, C., Zukowski, Z., Taylor, J., Pons, J.: Stable Audio\n493\tOpen. In: ICASSP 2025 - 2025 IEEE International Conference on Acoustics,\n494\tSpeech and Signal Processing (ICASSP), pp. 1–5 (2025)\n495\t[15] Fiebrink, R., Sonami, L.: Reflections on Eight Years of Instrument Creation with\n496\tMachine Learning. In: Proceedings of the International Conference on New\n497\tInterfaces for Musical Expression, pp. 237–242. Zenodo, Birmingham (2020)\n498\t[16] Fiebrink, R., Trueman, D., Cook, P.R.: A meta-instrument for interactive, on-thefly machine learning. In: Proceedings of the International Conference on New\n499\tInterfaces for Musical Expression, pp. 280–285. Pittsburgh, PA, United States\n500\t(2009)\n501\t[17] Giaccardi, E., Karana, E.: Foundations of Materials Experience: An Approach for\n502\tHCI. In: Proceedings of the 33rd Annual ACM Conference on Human Factors\n503\tin Computing Systems, CHI ’15, pp. 2447–2456. Association for Computing\n504\tMachinery, New York, NY, USA (2015)\n505\t[18] Gioti, A.M., Einbond, A., Born, G.: Composing the Assemblage: Probing Aesthetic and Technical Dimensions of Artistic Creation with Machine Learning.\n506\tComputer Music Journal 46(4), 62–80 (2022)\n507\t[19] Jack, R., Harrison, J., McPherson, A.: Digital Musical Instruments as Research\n508\tProducts. In: R. Michon, F. Schroeder (eds.) Proceedings of the International\n509\t\n510\t\f14\n511\t\n512\tSJ. Zheng et al.\n513\t\n514\tConference on New Interfaces for Musical Expression, pp. 446–451. Birmingham\n515\tCity University, Birmingham, UK (2020)\n516\t[20] Jordà, S.: Instruments and players: Some thoughts on digital lutherie. Journal of\n517\tNew Music Research 33(3), 321–341 (2004)\n518\t[21] McPherson, A.P., Chamberlain, A., Hazzard, A., McGrath, S., Benford, S.: Designing for Exploratory Play with a Hackable Digital Musical Instrument. In:\n519\tProceedings of the 2016 ACM Conference on Designing Interactive Systems, DIS\n520\t’16, pp. 1233–1245. Association for Computing Machinery, New York, NY, USA\n521\t(2016)\n522\t[22] Moore, T., Bradbury, J., Tremblay, P.A., Green, O.: Making Machine Learning\n523\tMusical: Reflections on a Year of Teaching FluCoMa. Journal SEAMUS 32,\n524\t24–38 (2025)\n525\t[23] Morrison, L., McPherson, A.: Entangling entanglement: a diffractive dialogue on\n526\tHCI and musical interactions. ACM (2024)\n527\t[24] Nabi, S., Esling, P., Peeters, G., Bevilacqua, F.: Embodied exploration of deep\n528\tlatent spaces in interactive dance-music performance. In: Proceedings of the 9th\n529\tInternational Conference on Movement and Computing, MOCO ’24. Association\n530\tfor Computing Machinery, New York, NY, USA (2024)\n531\t[25] Noel-Hirst, A., Saitis, C., Bryan-Kinns, N.: Sampling the Latent Space: Exploring\n532\tthe Creative Potential of Generative AI Through the Lens of Sample-Based Music\n533\tMaking (2025)\n534\t[26] Pasini, M., Lattner, S., Fazekas, G.: Music2Latent: Consistency Autoencoders for\n535\tLatent Audio Compression. In: Proceedings of the 25th International Society for\n536\tMusic Information Retrieval Conference, pp. 111–119. ISMIR (2024)\n537\t[27] Pelinski, T., McPherson, A., Fiebrink, R.: Ways of knowing, ways of writing:\n538\ttechnical practice research in new musical instrument design. Journal of New\n539\tMusic Research 53(0), 79–92 (2025)\n540\t[28] Privato, N., Shepardson, V., Lepri, G., Magnusson, T.: Stacco: Exploring the\n541\tEmbodied Perception of Latent Representations in Neural Synthesis. Proceedings\n542\tof the International Conference on New Interfaces for Musical Expression pp.\n543\t424–431 (2024)\n544\t[29] Saitis, C., Del Sette, B.M., Shier, J., Tian, H., Zheng, S., Skach, S., Reed, C.N.,\n545\tFord, C.: Timbre Tools: Ethnographic Perspectives on Timbre and Sonic Cultures\n546\tin Hackathon Designs. In: Proceedings of the 19th International Audio Mostly\n547\tConference: Explorations in Sonic Cultures, AM ’24, pp. 229–244. Association\n548\tfor Computing Machinery, New York, NY, USA (2024)\n549\t[30] Scurto, H., Caramiaux, B., Bevilacqua, F.: Prototyping Machine Learning Through\n550\tDiffractive Art Practice. In: Proceedings of the 2021 ACM Designing Interactive Systems Conference, DIS ’21, pp. 2013–2025. Association for Computing\n551\tMachinery, New York, NY, USA (2021)\n552\t[31] Sturdee, M., Genç, H.U., Wanick, V.: Diversifying Knowledge Production in\n553\tHCI: Exploring Materiality and Novel Formats for Scholarly Expression. In:\n554\tProceedings of the Eighteenth International Conference on Tangible, Embedded,\n555\tand Embodied Interaction, TEI ’24. Association for Computing Machinery, New\n556\tYork, NY, USA (2024)\n557\t\n558\t\fExplainable AI through the Lens of Material Agency\n559\t\n560\t15\n561\t\n562\t[32] Tholander, J., Normark, M., Rossitto, C.: Understanding agency in interaction\n563\tdesign materials. In: Proceedings of the SIGCHI Conference on Human Factors\n564\tin Computing Systems, CHI ’12, pp. 2499–2508. Association for Computing\n565\tMachinery, New York, NY, USA (2012)\n566\t[33] Tremblay, P.A., Green, O., Roma, G., Bradbury, J., Moore, T., Hart, J., Harker,\n567\tA.: The fluid corpus manipulation toolbox (2022)\n568\t[34] Tremblay, P.A., Roma, G., Green, O.: Enabling Programmatic Data Mining as\n569\tMusicking: The Fluid Corpus Manipulation Toolkit. Computer Music Journal\n570\t45(2), 9–23 (2021)\n571\t[35] Vallgårda, A., Boer, L., Tsaknaki, V., Svanæs, D.: Material Programming: a Design\n572\tPractice for Computational Composites. In: Proceedings of the 9th Nordic Conference on Human-Computer Interaction, NordiCHI ’16. Association for Computing\n573\tMachinery, New York, NY, USA (2016)\n574\t[36] Wiberg, M.: Material-Centered Interaction Design. In: The Materiality of Interaction: Notes on the Materials of Interaction Design. The MIT Press (2018)\n575\t[37] Wilson, E., Satomi, M., McLean, A., Schubert, D., Gonzalez, J.F.A.: Embodied\n576\tExploration of Latent Spaces and Explainable AI. In: Proceedings of Explainable\n577\tAI for the Arts Workshop 2024 (XAIxArts 2024) (2024)\n578\t[38] Xambó, A., Roma, G.: Human–machine agencies in live coding for music performance. Journal of New Music Research 53(1-2), 33–46 (2024)\n579\t[39] Yang, Q., Scuito, A., Zimmerman, J., Forlizzi, J., Steinfeld, A.: Investigating\n580\tHow Experienced UX Designers Effectively Work with Machine Learning. In:\n581\tProceedings of the 2018 Designing Interactive Systems Conference, DIS ’18, pp.\n582\t585–596. Association for Computing Machinery, New York, NY, USA (2018)\n583\t[40] Yee-King, M.: Latent Spaces: A Creative Approach. In: C. Vear, F. Poltronieri\n584\t(eds.) The Language of Creative AI: Practices, Aesthetics and Structures, pp.\n585\t137–154. Springer International Publishing, Cham (2022)\n586\t[41] Yoo, M., Ppali, S., Odom, W., Zhuang, Y., Kritika, K., Olson, W., Wieczorek, C.,\n587\tBiggs, H., Berger, A., Desjardins, A., Wakkary, R., Ringland, K.E.: Translating\n588\tHCI Research to Broader Audiences: Motivation, Inspiration, and Critical Factors\n589\ton Alternative Research Outcomes. In: Proceedings of the 2025 CHI Conference\n590\ton Human Factors in Computing Systems, CHI ’25. Association for Computing\n591\tMachinery, New York, NY, USA (2025)\n592\t[42] Zayas-Garin, L., Harrison, J., Jack, R., McPherson, A.: DMI Apprenticeship:\n593\tSharing and Replicating Musical Artefacts. In: Proceedings of the International\n594\tConference on New Interfaces for Musical Expression. Shanghai, China (2021)\n595\t[43] Zhang, S., Wang, H., Yi, X.: Exploring Collaboration Patterns and Strategies in\n596\tHuman-AI Co-creation through the Lens of Agency: A Scoping Review of the\n597\tTop-tier HCI Literature. Proc. ACM Hum.-Comput. Interact. 9(7) (2025)\n598\t[44] Zheng, S., Xambó Sedó, A., Bryan-Kinns, N.: A Mapping Strategy for Interacting\n599\twith Latent Audio Synthesis Using Artistic Materials. In: Proceedings of The\n600\tsecond international workshop on eXplainable AI for the Arts (XAIxArts 2)\n601\t(2024)\n602\t[45] Zheng, S.J., Xambó Sedó, A., Bryan-Kinns, N.: Exploring Gestural Affordances\n603\tin Audio Latent Space Navigation. Frontiers in Computer Science 7 (2025)\n604\t\n605\t\f\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "cc0d647a-2213-4dba-8074-a09f9c39c250", "uuid": "46f71943-3f03-49e4-815a-619df8e0eed9", "timestamp": "2026-07-28T09:37:35.261Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/paper_2607.txt", "content": "Explainable AI through the Lens of Material\nAgency: Enabling Musical Interface Design with\nNeural Audio Models\n\narXiv:2607.23309v1 [cs.HC] 25 Jul 2026\n\nShuoyang Jasper Zheng, Anna Xambó Sedó, and Nick Bryan-Kinns\n\nUnder review for “Explainable AI for the Arts” (N. Bryan-Kinns, Ed.), Springer.\nAbstract Recent work in Human-Computer Interaction (HCI) increasingly treats AI\nmodels as design materials that have distinctive computational properties to shape\ndesign artifacts. Artists learn to work with the model “at play” to explore their emerging\nproperties. The aim of explainability, in this view, is to make visible a crafting and\nhacking space to enable sustained creative practices with AI. In this chapter, we propose\nmaterial explainability as a range of activities and artifacts that transform AI models\ninto accessible and inclusive design materials in the workspace of artists, designers, and\nmakers. We present a case study of building a repository of resources to enable artistic\nexplorations of neural audio models in New Interfaces for Musical Expression (NIME)\ndesign. Reflecting on our community-building journey and the making of a collection\nof musical interface designs with a group of artists, we raise three recommendations\non enabling the exploration of AI as materials in artistic practices to inspire future XAI\ndesign for artists.\nKeywords AI as Material · Musical Interface Design · Neural Audio Models\n\n1 Introduction\nThe materiality of interaction design, in Human-Computer Interaction (HCI), emphasizes that the emergent characteristics of design materials can drive the design process\nShuoyang Jasper Zheng\nCentre for Digital Music, Queen Mary University of London, London, United Kingdom\ne-mail: shuoyang.zheng@qmul.ac.uk\nAnna Xambó Sedó\nCentre for Digital Music, Queen Mary University of London, London, United Kingdom\nNick Bryan-Kinns\nCreative Computing Institute, University of the Arts London, London, United Kingdom\n\n1\n\n\f2\n\nSJ. Zheng et al.\n\n[32]. Competent designers learn a rich material understanding of the grain, resistances,\nand propensities of materials in their workspace through hands-on practices, and develop\ntechnical know-how to work with them [36]. In recent discourse in AI and HCI, a trend\nof acknowledging AI models as design materials is taking place [30]. Technological\nadvances are thereby situated in creative practices in which designers probe, configure,\nand appropriate them as materials to unpack their creative possibilities [10], and craft\ninteractive experiences rather than solely focusing on engineering functionalities.\nThis material-oriented perspective offers a practice-based lens to XAIxArts, through\nwhich explainability can be conceived as working with AIs as materials to understand\nthem “at play”, through probing, configuring, and tweaking [3]. In this way, the audience for explainability is shifted to artists, designers, and makers, and the aim is to\nmake visible the crafting and hacking space for practice-based exploration with AIs.\nTherefore, we propose material explainability – that is, a range of activities or artifacts\nthat aim to transform AI models into accessible and inclusive design materials, to enable\nsustained creative practices with AI materials.\nIn this chapter, we discuss the intersection of explainability and the material-oriented\nengagement with AI models in creative practices. We reflect on a case study of New\nInterfaces for Musical Expression (NIME) designs with AI models as materials. The\ncase study focuses on a specific domain of machine learning that relies on explainability\n– the latent space, data representations learned by AI models that have typically been\ntreated as a “black box” that lacks explainability. We show how we build a repository\nof resources, including a software toolkit, documentation, tutorials, and a shared space\nfor projects to bring latent space into the workspace of artists, designers, and makers,\nand how they explore the materiality aspect of latent space to craft musical interfaces.\n\n2 Towards Explainability for Accessible and Inclusive AI Materials\nWe take the broader view of XAIxArts to refer to explainability as understanding\nhow to work with AI models in action in artistic contexts [37, 44]. In literature such\nas XAI in large language models [13], this way of gaining functional and operational\nunderstanding of the model, typically referred to as around the edges explanations, aims\nto “provide insights into a black box that can afford a wider range of actions” [13, p. 8]. In\nartistic practices with AIs, a similar “understanding models in action” approach has been\nwidely adopted. For instance, dancers exploring the model’s parameter space through\nembodied movements to relate its outputs to their actions [24], musicians creating\nannotations for sound-producing gestures to play the model as a musical instrument\n[45], or visual artists engaging in technical practice with the model to discover ways of\nsculpting artistic expressions [12]. A commonality, as described by Scurto et al. [30]\nusing the term “crafting machine learning”, is that artists seek in-depth engagement\nwith their AI models to discover practical necessities of how to work with them, beyond\na technical XAI lens. With this view of craft and design, and the iterative cycles of\nprobing, tuning, and programming models as computational material, it is reasonable\nto look at the “material turn” in HCI to unpack the role of explainability.\n\n\fExplainable AI through the Lens of Material Agency\n\n3\n\n2.1 The Material Turn and Material Agency\nThere has been a “material turn” in HCI and interaction design research over the\npast fifteen years [17] – that is, a material understanding of how distinctive properties\nof information technologies shape interaction experience and possibility. Influential\ncharacterizations of this material lens have guided research such as computational\ncomposites [35] which treat code, electronics, and physical matter as tightly coupled\ncomposites, and material-centered interaction design [36] in which designers learn a\nrepertoire of materials and their grain, resistances, and propensities through hands-on\nwork, in a similar way as they would work with wood, textiles, or sound. HCI researchers\nuse material agency to describe how emerging properties of materials actively contribute\nto the way a design activity unfolds. In this view, agency is “something that emerges and\nis dynamically enacted through practice” [32, p. 2506], rather than as a pre-existing set\nof affordances of a particular actor [4]. The notion of intra-action [2] is widely adopted\nto question the extreme localization of agency – either material dominates the human\nor human dominates the material – instead, musical interactions are co-produced in the\ndesigner’s entangled practice with their materials [23].\nIn recent discourse of AI in interaction design, a similar trend of acknowledging the\ncomputational properties of AI as materials is taking place, which we describe below.\n\n2.2 AI as Design Material\nScurto et al. [30] introduce elements of Machine Learning (ML) that constitute its\nmateriality, including techniques, data, models, and algorithms. They argue that artbased ML crafting can take these technical elements as an entry point for design, and\nemphasize on “the experience of raw computational properties through incremental\ncrafting” [30, p. 2022] rather than solely on engineering functionalities. The materiality\nof AI is not a singular form of infrastructure or software. Instead, it extends to a network\nof actors beyond just artists, audience, and the models [38].\nTherefore, the material agency of AI systems in art and design has been described as a\nheterogeneous assemblage that involves a range of “technically, materially, socially, and\ntemporally” distributed infrastructures [18]. To be clear, one strand of research in HCI on\nhuman-AI co-creation focuses on explicitly assigning agency to either the AI or human\n[43]. This contrasts the intra-action relationship in which AI models are understood\nas material rather than co-creator [32], and the material agency only emerges when\nbeing surfaced, negotiated, and repurposed. Acknowledging the material agency of AI\nmodels means attending to how their affordances, tendencies, and constraints participate\nin shaping the artifacts and aesthetic commitments, and being able to design, craft, and\nrefine them across time [30].\nRepositioning AI in this material agency perspective implies that its characteristic\nbehaviors are not merely technical constraints to be overcome, but affordances that\ndesigners must learn to work with through practice [39]. In this respect, Pelinski et al.\n\n\f4\n\nSJ. Zheng et al.\n\n[27, p. 10] propose technical practice research to account for the material agency in\nworks that are surrounded by technical development:\nWe often do exist in a more dialogic process with our technical practice, in which we converse\nwith our materials, and as such, finding space for practice research might allow us to articulate\nknowledge about how (rather than what) we do.\n\nTechnical practice research argues that the material-oriented perspective can be\nneglected when knowledge production is driven by searching for insights and achieving\ngoals. In practice, the real-time and reflexive accounts during the technical development\nphase play an important role in shedding light on the temporal emergence of material\nagency [27]. The issue of linear problem-solving narratives in practice research mirrors\nthe challenge of XAI in the Arts [7]. In particular, technical explanations are typically\nframed as goal and productivity oriented, raising the barrier to appropriating them for\ncreative and artistic practices [6].\n\n2.3 Accessible and Inclusive XAI Materials\nIn the landscape of XAIxArts, attention has been drawn to the accessible, inclusive,\ninterdisciplinary practice with AI [7]. This echoes the interest in diversifying dissemination methods in HCI [31], which argues that traditional academic publications as a\nway of sharing research findings can exclude wider audiences. Yoo et al. [41] propose\nAlternative Research Outcomes to challenge the one-size-fits-all research dissemination\nstrategies. They explore situated and dedicated research activities or artifacts, such as\ndocumentaries, public exhibitions, multimedia artifacts, as “new endpoints to translate,\ncommunicate, or disseminate research insights in an accessible and engaging form\ntailored to the intended audience” [41].\nPractitioners in the XAIxArts community also adopt the view of making explainability engaging to audiences with a range of technical literacy. This is reflected as an AI\nas Material perspective [5] in which the AI models themselves can be artworks. Artistled practices such as probing model behaviors, exposing bias, and re-creating glitches\nemerge to enable engagement with a wider audience. In this way, research insights on\nmodel explainability are made accessible through alternative research outcomes such\nas exhibitions, interactive experiences, or artistic installations.\nThe XAIxArts discourse on exploring the AI itself as artistic pieces poses open\nquestions on where the creative agency resides [5, p. 45], aiming to answer “who is\nresponsible for the explanation: the AI model, the artist, or the audience?” Here, we\nsuggest that it is desirable to consider the dissemination of models into the hands of\nartists. Therefore, the material explainability aims to answer how to enable artists to\ndevelop technical know-how and technical practices with AI materials. A similar notion\nof material explainability was raised in the XAIxArts Manifesto [7], which calls for\n“demystifying the computational materiality of AI in accessible languages and engaging\nformats” for inclusive dissemination of AI as material.\n\n\fExplainable AI through the Lens of Material Agency\n\n5\n\nFollowing this call for material explainability in a concrete creative-technical setting,\nwe focus on neural audio models in musical interface design, where AI models are both\na key site of creative potential and a barrier to hands-on engagement.\n\n2.4 Neural Audio Models in New Interfaces for Musical Expression\nTo unpack this theme and illustrate the role of explainability in exploring the materiality\nof AI, we look at a specific domain of generative AI for sound synthesis that is typically\nconsidered to lack explainability – the latent space. In AI for sound analysis and\nsynthesis, the latent space is a compact multi-dimensional map learned by neural audio\nmodels. A typical implementation of neural audio models is an autoencoder, in which an\nencoder compresses audio signals into a latent space, and a decoder reconstructs audio\nfrom latent representations. This method of learning audio data allows them to capture\ncomplex timbral and spectral characteristics that are difficult to specify explicitly. We\ndirect intrigued readers to the chapter by Yee-King [40] for a comprehensive and\ninclusive explanation of using latent spaces in creative practices.\nIn New Interfaces for Musical Expression (NIME), neural audio models open new\nopportunities by repositioning sound synthesis as a matter of interacting with the learned\nlatent representations. For instance, the latent space walk approach of mapping user\ninputs into latent spaces enables designers to craft novel musical instruments [28, 45].\nThis shifts neural audio models from a technical artifact into design materials that can\nbe explored, used, and appropriated.\nDespite the creative possibilities it opens, one of the long-standing criticisms of\nlatent spaces is the absence of easy-to-understand musical attributes encoded across\nall dimensions. Consequently, the ways of navigating the latent space and relating the\nsound outputs rely on the designer’s sense-making. Therefore, to explore neural audio\nmodels in NIME design, designers seek to understand the model’s computational properties through iterative exploration and configuration. However, technical AI research\nis typically positioned outside creative coding and audio production environments, and\nlimited learning resources raise the barrier to hands-on engagement with the model\n[11]. In the next section, we present our journey of transforming neural audio models\ninto situated and easy-to-integrate materials in the hands of artists and designers.\n\n3 Crafting Musical Interactions with AI Audio Latent Space\nThis section describes a case study of building a community of resources, including\nsoftware tools, documentation, tutorials, and demo projects, to turn neural audio models\ninto explainable materials for designers. We introduce the Latent Terrain toolkit, a\npackage of Max/MSP objects to build customized interactive sound spaces for neural\naudio autoencoders. We acknowledge that the appropriation of technical artifacts can\nbe open-ended and cannot be contained by a singular effort. Therefore, we hope to use\nour project only as a heuristic for further work, rather than an ultimate solution.\n\n\f6\n\nSJ. Zheng et al.\n\nFig. 1 We held a series of workshops in which musicians were invited to explore the DMI probe\n(Right). They were tasked to create musical scores to re-enact sound pieces (Left).\n\n3.1 Description of the Research Project\nLatent Terrain is the output of the first author’s doctoral research project conducted\nbetween 2024 and 2025 [45], at the intersection of NIME, XAI, and HCI. The research\nlooked at the embodied music cognition aspect of movement-sound interaction in\naudio latent spaces, investigating how musicians explore the latent space as a musical\ninstrument and the performing techniques that emerged. A full technical account of the\nproject is beyond the scope of this paper, we therefore invite readers to our previous work\nfor a detailed journey of the research [45]. A brief narrative of the journey includes:\n1. The research took the method of Digital Musical Instruments (DMIs) as probes [19],\nin which musical instruments are built to serve as a means to explore musicians’\nsubjective experience.\n2. We crafted a DMI with a stylus and tablet interface (shown in Figure 1) that allows\nmusicians to navigate the latent space by operating the stylus, and hear the real-time\nsound synthesized by the autoencoder.\n3. Latent Terrain was developed as a deep learning method to interactively map the\nlatent space of an autoencoder to a 2-dimensional control space, using a customized\naudio corpora. To facilitate rapid prototyping and testing of the mapping, we developed a toolkit in the Max/MSP visual programming environment that allows us to\nintegrate the Latent Terrain model and the autoencoder together into the DMI.\n\n3.2 Bridging Computational Materiality to Accessible Platforms\nInitially, the Latent Terrain toolkit was published as supplementary material in a research\narticle [45]. As the project progressed, the intended audience shifted from academic\nresearchers to broader DMI makers in the field of AI audio synthesis. This motivated\n\n\fExplainable AI through the Lens of Material Agency\n\n7\n\nFig. 2 A collection of materials to disseminate the research aspect of Latent Terrain to hands-on\nexperience in Max/MSP: (A) Online documentation, (B) video tutorials, (C and D) example patches\nas part of a getting-started guide.\n\nus to maintain the toolkit as a public Max/MSP (a visual programming language for\nmusic and multimedia) package 1 to allow others to create their own instruments,\nrather than offering a static musical instrument with a fixed mode of interaction. In\nthis way, the Latent Terrain system became a computational material, working together\nwith autoencoders, customized audio corpora, and interfaces, in the ecology of musical\ninteractions design.\nThe intention of the project, therefore, became transforming the technical system into\nan accessible and easy-to-integrate material in DMI design. We packaged the mapping\nmodel into a collection of Max/MSP externals, offering a Max-style API that supports\nthe workflow of model creation and inference. The API design was situated and guided\nby the first author’s practice of DMI design and composing with autoencoders, which\nwe reported in another publication [45]. In summary, we reflected on a long-term selfuse of the system in a series of interface designs to identify the practical necessities in\nthe workflow of integrating the latent space into DMI. The aim was to expose proper\nconfigurations to the API to allow for customizations and extended uses of the system,\nwhile maintaining simplicity to avoid technical knowledge becoming a barrier.\n1 Our Latent Terrain Toolkit: https://jasper-zheng.github.io/nn_terrain/\n\n\f8\n\nSJ. Zheng et al.\n\nTo share our technical know-how gained throughout the development process, we\nmaintain an online repository2 of documentation and tutorials as a form of alternative\nresearch outcomes [41], shown in Figure 2. The intended audience is audio programmers, musicians, DMI makers, and broader communities of creative practitioners. The\nonline documentation provides an introduction to the package and a getting-started\nguide, using inclusive language to explain the inner architecture of autoencoders and\nentry points to unpack them. It also contains step-by-step instructions with video tutorials to cover a range of use cases of the package, from basic workflows such as\nloading and visualizing pre-trained mapping models, to in-depth customization such as\ncreating one’s own mapping model and experimenting with other input devices. On the\nMax/MSP side, each object in the package offers a help file containing a walkthrough of\nits functionalities, explanations of the API and configurations, and working examples\nof how it can be used in a patch.\nWe also maintain an open repository of autoencoders to enrich the pool of materials\nfor the designers (links to these resources can be found on our online documentation\npage). This is motivated by the gap between research on autoencoders and their practical\navailability in musical platforms. The Realtime Audio Variational autoEncoder (RAVE)\n[8] model is widely adopted by practitioners in NIME due to its availability to be used\nwith the nn∼3 external in Max/MSP, and several other creative coding platforms, for\nreal-time inference. However, a range of other neural audio autoencoders is exclusive to\nresearch environments, and can lack the consideration of real-time streamability at the\ndevelopment stage [9]. We added two other open-source autoencoders (Music2Latent\n[26] and the autoencoder in the Stable Audio Open 1.0 model [14]) that vary in configurations and model architecture, and implemented them to the nn∼ external. We also\nmade available a collection of RAVE models trained by the first author using Creative\nCommons datasets. This repository of resources aims to encourage a plural repertoire\nof materials, and to bridge the capability of recent research in neural audio synthesis to\ncreative coding environments.\n\n3.3 Community Building with Artists\nTo further encourage artistic exploration of the repository we created, we took a\ncommunity-driven approach of allowing artists, designers, and digital luthiers to share\ntheir projects and technical know-how. The benefit of communities that intersect researchers, academic knowledge, and audiences has been recognised in various art and\ntechnology projects, such as the interactive machine learning tool Wekinator [15], the\ncorpora manipulation toolkit FluCoMa [34, 33]. Inspired by these works, we create a\nspace on our website 4 for blog posts and project showcases, and invited a group of\nartists to explore a key question of “how do neural audio models open a domain of\ndesign in musical interfaces”.\n2 https://jasper-zheng.github.io/nn_terrain/\n3 https://github.com/acids-ircam/nn_tilde\n4 https://jasper-zheng.github.io/nn_terrain/posts/\n\n\fExplainable AI through the Lens of Material Agency\n\n9\n\nFig. 3 A collection of projects made using the Latent Terrain toolkit: (A) A MaxMSP interface for using\nvisual sketches to steer a RAVE model; (B) A step sequencer that plays short “latent samples” while\nallowing users to adapt the terrain sound space on the fly. (C) nn/mémoire by Jiatong Liu, an audiovisual virtual gallery with a theme of capturing the sound of cultural heritage in Hutongs (traditional\nnorthern Chinese courtyard houses in Beijing); (D) Trek by Nico Garcı́a-Peguinho and Nikhil Bullock, a\ncollaborative composition and live performance system exploring sampling and interpolation strategies\nfor the latent space; (E) Repressive Terrain by Keigo Yoshida, an experimental data sonification patch\nwith a theme of active interventions in a focused meditation; (F) ambient terrain 1 by Dan Hearn, an\nambient soundtrack exploring the combination of AI and non-AI materials in the sample-based music\nproduction workflow.\n\nFigure 3 shows the collection of works in the repository. The first two projects (A\nand B), created by the authors as an initiation to explore the design space. The other\nfour projects (C to F) are contributed by four artists who shared a mutual interest\nin exploring neural audio synthesis. Each project in the collection took a different\napproach to musical interface design, ranging from an immersive virtual gallery, a live\nperformance, a brain–computer interaction, and an ambient soundscape composition.\n\n\f10\n\nSJ. Zheng et al.\n\nHere we share reflections from the four artists to gain insight into their approach\nto understanding the models during their creation process. Quotes are taken from our\nconversations with each artist during the creation of the collection.\nVarious artists acknowledged the uniqueness of working with neural audio models\nby comparing them to conventional sound synthesis algorithms. For instance, Jiatong\nLiu, a creative technologist in machine learning, composition, and film, the creator\nof nn/mémoire, made a comparison of neural audio models and conventional music\nproduction tools such as reverb or EQ: “[neural audio models] shape a sound by entirely\nchanging the structure and order of it”. Keigo Yoshida, an artist and scientist, the\ncreator of Repressive Terrain, emphasizes the interactive adaptation of sound spaces as\nan important iterative way of customising and working with the AI material. He made\nan analogy of adding training data to the Latent Terrain system with adding materials\nto an audio sampler, but “it is able to shift timbre and musical structure across genres”.\nNico Garcı́a-Peguinho, an artist and researcher in machine listening and algorithmic\ncomposition, the co-creator of Trek, highlighted that “[neural audio synthesis] feels less\nintuitive than conventional oscillator-based synthesis”.\nArtists also mention the importance of learning from trials and errors. Dan Hearn,\na creative technologist and musician, the creator of ambient terrain 1, describes his\nworkflow with autoencoders for soundscape composition as “explorative and serendipitous,” emphasising that the autoencoder “gradually finds its place” within his existing\nsample-based practice through repeated trials, rather than being adopted as a standalone instrument with fixed affordances. Jiatong describes how working with neural\naudio models demands a shift in composition approach: “need to learn to deal with the\nunpredictability through trial and error, without fully eliminating uncertainty”. Nico\nalso highlighted that a tacit understanding of how to work with the material was made\nlegible through active and embodied listening in the sound space. Keigo described the\n“tracing the map like a musical score” as a compositional method that is difficult to\narticulate without hands-on engagement.\nThere is also a demand for tools and facilities to support the hands-on engagement\nwith neural audio models. For instance, Nico mentioned that conventional sound synthesis methods, such as sample-based synthesis or oscillator-based synthesis, benefit\nfrom a rich set of tools such as visualisation or preset saving and loading, whereas for\nneural audio models, “very often you have to build things yourself, which makes them\nharder to work with”.\n\n4 Enabling Artists’ Exploration of AI Materials\nBased on the development process of the Latent Terrain package, the online resources,\nand the reflections from the artists, here we discuss three takeaways on how to transform\nAI models into accessible and inclusive XAI materials in the crafting space of NIME,\nto inspire future XAI design and development.\nFirst, make visible how AI models can be tailored into the material assemblage of different artists’ practices. In NIME design, designers work with a range\nof synthesis algorithms, sensors, and the listening and composing practice, forming an\n\n\fExplainable AI through the Lens of Material Agency\n\n11\n\nassemblage [18]. Composing and weaving together a set of co-functioning materials in\nthe assemblage is through the rapid cycles of trial and adjustment. Reflecting on the four\ncreative projects, this iterative way of exploring and integrating materials is enabled\nby conditions in which they can probe, tune, and configure the AI model. In this way,\nthe role of autoencoders in creative practices became clear through the connection and\ninteraction with other materials in the design space, rather than through predetermined\naffordances. In contrast to AI models that are constrained in technical research environments or API calls, the Latent Terrain toolkit unpacks the latent space into a creative\ncoding environment, in which the autoencoder can belong to a broad constellation of\nAI and non-AI materials. The use and appropriation of AI as material can therefore be\nexplored and understood by creative makers.\nSecond, enable artists to learn technical and practical know-how of AI models\nthrough crafting. DMI design, as a creative practice, is interdisciplinary technical work\nthat involves apprenticeships and learning curves [42]. Technical practice research is\ntermed to relocate knowledge production in first-person, real-time technical practices\n[27]. This echoes the view of material-centered design, in which material understanding\nis built through close and iterative engagement, and enables skills of weaving together\nmaterials to fulfill a piece of design [36].\nExplainability, in this respect, is not a one-time account of the model’s inner work\nthat will be disposed of immediately after the generation, but resources that can ensure\nmakers’ sustained practices [11] with the AI material. Within the four artistic pieces,\nmakers do not shy away from tuning and troubleshooting their AI models. Instead,\nthe complex and technical nature of latent space is acknowledged, but as described by\nJiatong, they “learn to deal with the unpredictability”, and by Nico, they “seed help from\nthe documentation to learn things like visualizing the latent parameters”. Crucially, this\nform of explainability is described as tacit understanding [1] that is uniquely attached\nbetween the maker and the material [21]. This makes explainability inseparable from\nhands-on experimentation and listening to enable technique learning.\nThird, foster exchanges of domain-specific techniques and know-how among\nartists through community-building. In creative practices, techniques and knowledge\nemerge when communities can repeatedly return to the same material to negotiate and\ncollaborate [29]. This ocio-material environments [25] is enabled by allowing practitioners to establish domain-specific vocabularies to share experiences and techniques.\nFor instance, the account of the turntable is an example of how sustained practice and\nmusical culture transform a technical artifact into a playable medium [20]. In the domain\nof AI for creativity, various works argue that “providing the tools is not enough” [22].\nInstead, the long-term and techno-fluent engagements with AI as material are enabled\nby shareable resources for learning and pedagogy [16, 22]. Within the practices with\nthe Latent Terrain toolkit, artists discover their ways of knowing and working that can\ninfluence other practitioners. For instance, Nico’s use of “active listening” method and\nDan’s use of “latent resampling” to characterize his workflow. This community aspect\nof explainability focuses on how the tacit knowledge of crafting and working can be\narticulated and circulated through communities to ensure a sustained practice with AI\nmaterials.\n\n\f12\n\nSJ. Zheng et al.\n\n5 Conclusion\nIn this chapter, we suggest that XAI for creative practice can be approached as material\nexplainability: a range of activities and artifacts that aim to bring AI models into the\nworkspace as accessible and inclusive materials for design. Using latent space in neural\naudio models as a case study, we described how the Latent Terrain toolkit translates a\ntechnically complex and opaque representational space in AI models into a repository\nof software tools, documentation, resources, and tutorials for artists. Based on the\nrepository and the reflection from a community of artists using it, we show how the\nmaterial explainability approach supports hands-on artistic explorations of neural audio\nmodels in NIME design. We concluded by raising three suggestions on future XAI\ndesign for artistic practices: enable crafting and hacking by making visible how AI\nmodels can be tailored into different artists’ practices; enable artists to learn technical\nand practical know-how of AI models through crafting; foster exchanges of domainspecific techniques and know-how among artists through community-building.\nAcknowledgements Shuoyang Zheng is a research student supported by the EPSRC UKRI Centre\nfor Doctoral Training in Artificial Intelligence and Music (grant number EP/S022694/1). We thank all\nfour artists for their engagement with the project and their contributions of their project descriptions,\ngraphic content, and blog posts on the project webpage.\n\nEthics Approval The project was approved by the Queen Mary University of London ethics committee,\nreference number: QMERC20.565.DSEECS25.026.\n\nReferences\n[1] Abuzuraiq, A.M., Pasquier, P.: Explainability-in-Action: Enabling Expressive Manipulation and Tacit Understanding by Bending Diffusion Models in ComfyUI.\nIn: Proceedings of Explainable AI for the Arts Workshop 2025 (XAIxArts 2025)\n(2025)\n[2] Barad, K.: Meeting the Universe Halfway: Quantum Physics and the Entanglement\nof Matter and Meaning. Duke University Press (2007)\n[3] Benjamin, J.J., Berger, A., Merrill, N., Pierce, J.: Machine Learning Uncertainty as\na Design Material: A Post-Phenomenological Inquiry. In: Proceedings of the 2021\nCHI Conference on Human Factors in Computing Systems, CHI ’21. Association\nfor Computing Machinery, New York, NY, USA (2021)\n[4] Bown, O., Eldridge, A., Mccormack, J.: Understanding interaction in contemporary digital music: From instruments to behavioural objects. Organised Sound\n14(2), 188–196 (2009)\n[5] Bryan-Kinns, N.: Reflections on Explainable AI for the Arts (XAIxArts). Interactions 31(1), 43–47 (2024)\n\n\fExplainable AI through the Lens of Material Agency\n\n13\n\n[6] Bryan-Kinns, N., Li, Z.: Reducing Barriers to the Use of Marginalised Music\nGenres in AI. In: Proceedings of Explainable AI for the Arts Workshop 2024\n(XAIxArts 2024) (2024)\n[7] Bryan-Kinns, N., Zheng, S.J., Castro, F., Lewis, M., Chang, J.R., Vigliensoni, G.,\nBroad, T., Clemens, M.P., Wilson, E.: XAIxArts Manifesto: Explainable AI for\nthe Arts. In: Proceedings of the Extended Abstracts of the CHI Conference on\nHuman Factors in Computing Systems, CHI EA ’25. Association for Computing\nMachinery, New York, NY, USA (2025)\n[8] Caillon, A., Esling, P.: RAVE: A variational autoencoder for fast and high-quality\nneural audio synthesis (2021)\n[9] Caillon, A., Esling, P.: Streamable Neural Audio Synthesis With Non-Causal\nConvolutions (2022)\n[10] Caramiaux, B., Fdili Alaoui, S.: “Explorers of Unknown Planets”: Practices and\nPolitics of Artificial Intelligence in Visual Arts. Proc. ACM Hum.-Comput.\nInteract. 6(CSCW2) (2022)\n[11] Clarke, I., Rı́, F.A.D., Masu, R.: Longevity of Deep Generative Models in NIME:\nChallenges and Practices for Reactivation. In: D. Cavdir, F. Berthaut (eds.) Proceedings of the International Conference on New Interfaces for Musical Expression, pp. 224–230. Canberra, Australia (2025)\n[12] Cole, A., Grierson, M.: Kiss/Crash: Using Diffusion Models to Explore Real\nDesire in the Shadow of Artificial Representations. Proc. ACM Comput. Graph.\nInteract. Tech. 6(2) (2023)\n[13] Ehsan, U., Riedl, M.: Explainable AI Reloaded: Challenging the XAI Status Quo\nin the Era of Large Language Models. In: Proceedings of the Halfway to the\nFuture Symposium, HttF ’24. Association for Computing Machinery, New York,\nNY, USA (2024)\n[14] Evans, Z., Parker, J.D., Carr, C., Zukowski, Z., Taylor, J., Pons, J.: Stable Audio\nOpen. In: ICASSP 2025 - 2025 IEEE International Conference on Acoustics,\nSpeech and Signal Processing (ICASSP), pp. 1–5 (2025)\n[15] Fiebrink, R., Sonami, L.: Reflections on Eight Years of Instrument Creation with\nMachine Learning. In: Proceedings of the International Conference on New\nInterfaces for Musical Expression, pp. 237–242. Zenodo, Birmingham (2020)\n[16] Fiebrink, R., Trueman, D., Cook, P.R.: A meta-instrument for interactive, on-thefly machine learning. In: Proceedings of the International Conference on New\nInterfaces for Musical Expression, pp. 280–285. Pittsburgh, PA, United States\n(2009)\n[17] Giaccardi, E., Karana, E.: Foundations of Materials Experience: An Approach for\nHCI. In: Proceedings of the 33rd Annual ACM Conference on Human Factors\nin Computing Systems, CHI ’15, pp. 2447–2456. Association for Computing\nMachinery, New York, NY, USA (2015)\n[18] Gioti, A.M., Einbond, A., Born, G.: Composing the Assemblage: Probing Aesthetic and Technical Dimensions of Artistic Creation with Machine Learning.\nComputer Music Journal 46(4), 62–80 (2022)\n[19] Jack, R., Harrison, J., McPherson, A.: Digital Musical Instruments as Research\nProducts. In: R. Michon, F. Schroeder (eds.) Proceedings of the International\n\n\f14\n\nSJ. Zheng et al.\n\nConference on New Interfaces for Musical Expression, pp. 446–451. Birmingham\nCity University, Birmingham, UK (2020)\n[20] Jordà, S.: Instruments and players: Some thoughts on digital lutherie. Journal of\nNew Music Research 33(3), 321–341 (2004)\n[21] McPherson, A.P., Chamberlain, A., Hazzard, A., McGrath, S., Benford, S.: Designing for Exploratory Play with a Hackable Digital Musical Instrument. In:\nProceedings of the 2016 ACM Conference on Designing Interactive Systems, DIS\n’16, pp. 1233–1245. Association for Computing Machinery, New York, NY, USA\n(2016)\n[22] Moore, T., Bradbury, J., Tremblay, P.A., Green, O.: Making Machine Learning\nMusical: Reflections on a Year of Teaching FluCoMa. Journal SEAMUS 32,\n24–38 (2025)\n[23] Morrison, L., McPherson, A.: Entangling entanglement: a diffractive dialogue on\nHCI and musical interactions. ACM (2024)\n[24] Nabi, S., Esling, P., Peeters, G., Bevilacqua, F.: Embodied exploration of deep\nlatent spaces in interactive dance-music performance. In: Proceedings of the 9th\nInternational Conference on Movement and Computing, MOCO ’24. Association\nfor Computing Machinery, New York, NY, USA (2024)\n[25] Noel-Hirst, A., Saitis, C., Bryan-Kinns, N.: Sampling the Latent Space: Exploring\nthe Creative Potential of Generative AI Through the Lens of Sample-Based Music\nMaking (2025)\n[26] Pasini, M., Lattner, S., Fazekas, G.: Music2Latent: Consistency Autoencoders for\nLatent Audio Compression. In: Proceedings of the 25th International Society for\nMusic Information Retrieval Conference, pp. 111–119. ISMIR (2024)\n[27] Pelinski, T., McPherson, A., Fiebrink, R.: Ways of knowing, ways of writing:\ntechnical practice research in new musical instrument design. Journal of New\nMusic Research 53(0), 79–92 (2025)\n[28] Privato, N., Shepardson, V., Lepri, G., Magnusson, T.: Stacco: Exploring the\nEmbodied Perception of Latent Representations in Neural Synthesis. Proceedings\nof the International Conference on New Interfaces for Musical Expression pp.\n424–431 (2024)\n[29] Saitis, C., Del Sette, B.M., Shier, J., Tian, H., Zheng, S., Skach, S., Reed, C.N.,\nFord, C.: Timbre Tools: Ethnographic Perspectives on Timbre and Sonic Cultures\nin Hackathon Designs. In: Proceedings of the 19th International Audio Mostly\nConference: Explorations in Sonic Cultures, AM ’24, pp. 229–244. Association\nfor Computing Machinery, New York, NY, USA (2024)\n[30] Scurto, H., Caramiaux, B., Bevilacqua, F.: Prototyping Machine Learning Through\nDiffractive Art Practice. In: Proceedings of the 2021 ACM Designing Interactive Systems Conference, DIS ’21, pp. 2013–2025. Association for Computing\nMachinery, New York, NY, USA (2021)\n[31] Sturdee, M., Genç, H.U., Wanick, V.: Diversifying Knowledge Production in\nHCI: Exploring Materiality and Novel Formats for Scholarly Expression. In:\nProceedings of the Eighteenth International Conference on Tangible, Embedded,\nand Embodied Interaction, TEI ’24. Association for Computing Machinery, New\nYork, NY, USA (2024)\n\n\fExplainable AI through the Lens of Material Agency\n\n15\n\n[32] Tholander, J., Normark, M., Rossitto, C.: Understanding agency in interaction\ndesign materials. In: Proceedings of the SIGCHI Conference on Human Factors\nin Computing Systems, CHI ’12, pp. 2499–2508. Association for Computing\nMachinery, New York, NY, USA (2012)\n[33] Tremblay, P.A., Green, O., Roma, G., Bradbury, J., Moore, T., Hart, J., Harker,\nA.: The fluid corpus manipulation toolbox (2022)\n[34] Tremblay, P.A., Roma, G., Green, O.: Enabling Programmatic Data Mining as\nMusicking: The Fluid Corpus Manipulation Toolkit. Computer Music Journal\n45(2), 9–23 (2021)\n[35] Vallgårda, A., Boer, L., Tsaknaki, V., Svanæs, D.: Material Programming: a Design\nPractice for Computational Composites. In: Proceedings of the 9th Nordic Conference on Human-Computer Interaction, NordiCHI ’16. Association for Computing\nMachinery, New York, NY, USA (2016)\n[36] Wiberg, M.: Material-Centered Interaction Design. In: The Materiality of Interaction: Notes on the Materials of Interaction Design. The MIT Press (2018)\n[37] Wilson, E., Satomi, M., McLean, A., Schubert, D., Gonzalez, J.F.A.: Embodied\nExploration of Latent Spaces and Explainable AI. In: Proceedings of Explainable\nAI for the Arts Workshop 2024 (XAIxArts 2024) (2024)\n[38] Xambó, A., Roma, G.: Human–machine agencies in live coding for music performance. Journal of New Music Research 53(1-2), 33–46 (2024)\n[39] Yang, Q., Scuito, A., Zimmerman, J., Forlizzi, J., Steinfeld, A.: Investigating\nHow Experienced UX Designers Effectively Work with Machine Learning. In:\nProceedings of the 2018 Designing Interactive Systems Conference, DIS ’18, pp.\n585–596. Association for Computing Machinery, New York, NY, USA (2018)\n[40] Yee-King, M.: Latent Spaces: A Creative Approach. In: C. Vear, F. Poltronieri\n(eds.) The Language of Creative AI: Practices, Aesthetics and Structures, pp.\n137–154. Springer International Publishing, Cham (2022)\n[41] Yoo, M., Ppali, S., Odom, W., Zhuang, Y., Kritika, K., Olson, W., Wieczorek, C.,\nBiggs, H., Berger, A., Desjardins, A., Wakkary, R., Ringland, K.E.: Translating\nHCI Research to Broader Audiences: Motivation, Inspiration, and Critical Factors\non Alternative Research Outcomes. In: Proceedings of the 2025 CHI Conference\non Human Factors in Computing Systems, CHI ’25. Association for Computing\nMachinery, New York, NY, USA (2025)\n[42] Zayas-Garin, L., Harrison, J., Jack, R., McPherson, A.: DMI Apprenticeship:\nSharing and Replicating Musical Artefacts. In: Proceedings of the International\nConference on New Interfaces for Musical Expression. Shanghai, China (2021)\n[43] Zhang, S., Wang, H., Yi, X.: Exploring Collaboration Patterns and Strategies in\nHuman-AI Co-creation through the Lens of Agency: A Scoping Review of the\nTop-tier HCI Literature. Proc. ACM Hum.-Comput. Interact. 9(7) (2025)\n[44] Zheng, S., Xambó Sedó, A., Bryan-Kinns, N.: A Mapping Strategy for Interacting\nwith Latent Audio Synthesis Using Artistic Materials. In: Proceedings of The\nsecond international workshop on eXplainable AI for the Arts (XAIxArts 2)\n(2024)\n[45] Zheng, S.J., Xambó Sedó, A., Bryan-Kinns, N.: Exploring Gestural Affordances\nin Audio Latent Space Navigation. Frontiers in Computer Science 7 (2025)\n\n\f", "numLines": 605, "startLine": 1, "totalLines": 605}}}
